'''Shimabara Prefecture''' ({{lang|ja|島原県}}, ''Shimabara-ken'') is a prefecture of [[Okaiken]] located in the volcanic highlands of the island of [[Ishijima]], occupying the deep interior between the western and eastern coasts. Its population as of 2026 was 1,220,888, with approximately 37% concentrated in the prefectural capital of [[Shimabara, Shimabara|Shimabara]] (pop. 493,588). Shimabara is one of the most mountainous and sparsely populated prefectures on Ishijima, a land of active volcanoes, hot springs, and the pure mountain streams that produce Okaiken's finest [[wasabi]].

The prefecture's name—{{nihongo|島原|Shimabara}}, meaning "island origin"—refers to the volcanic origin of the land itself. The [[Shimabara Volcanic Group]] ({{lang|ja|島原火山群}}, ''Shimabara-kazangun''), a chain of active and dormant volcanoes that forms the spine of the prefecture, has shaped every aspect of Shimabara's geography, economy, and culture. The volcanic soils produce the mineral-rich water that sustains the wasabi terraces, the geothermal heat that powers the onsen resorts, and the dramatic landscape of craters, lava fields, and emerald crater lakes that draws hikers and geologists from across Fosia.

== History ==

=== Ancient and pre-shogunate period ===
The Shimabara highlands were among the last areas of Ishijima to be permanently settled, due to the volcanic activity and the rugged terrain. The [[Atsu]] communities who eventually established themselves in the interior developed a distinctive culture adapted to the volcanic environment, cultivating crops in the fertile ash soils and using the hot springs for cooking, bathing, and the natural fermentation of foods.

The volcanic streams of the Shimabara highlands were found to be ideal for [[wasabi]] cultivation as early as the 8th century CE. The Atsu highland communities developed the {{nihongo|'''Shimabara wasabi terrace'''|島原山葵棚田|Shimabara-wasabi tanada}}—a system of stone-walled, terraced pools fed by gravity-directed volcanic spring water—that remains the basis of wasabi production in the prefecture to this day. The terraces, which cascade down the mountain streams in stepped formations, are among the most distinctive agricultural landscapes in Okaiken and have been designated a [[National Scenic Monument (Okaiken)|National Scenic Monument]].

=== The Honda Shogunate and the Shimabara Domain ===
The [[Shimabara Domain]] ({{lang|ja|島原藩}}, ''Shimabara-han'') was established under the [[Honda Shogunate]] as a retainer territory of the [[Hinoemata clan]], hereditary vassals of the Honda who controlled the volcanic interior. The domain's wealth was built on wasabi and sake—the pure volcanic spring water produced a crisp, clean sake that was prized at the shogunal court, and the Shimabara wasabi was considered the finest in Okaiken.

The Shogunate period also saw the development of the [[Shimabara Onsen]] resort culture. The volcanic hot springs of the interior, which had been used by the Atsu communities for centuries, were developed into formal ''ryokan'' resorts that attracted the shogunal aristocracy. The [[Nosegawa Onsen]], the oldest continuously operating hot spring resort in the prefecture, was established in 1428 and remains one of the most prestigious onsen destinations in Okaiken.

=== Modern era ===
The [[Saito Restoration]] of 1871 converted the Shimabara Domain into Shimabara Prefecture. The volcanic terrain initially limited industrial development, but the 20th century saw the prefecture find its economic niche in premium agriculture and geothermal tourism. The [[Shimabara Wasabi Cooperative]], established in 1924, coordinated the modernisation of the wasabi terraces and the expansion of production to meet the growing national demand.

The [[Shimabara Volcanic Eruption of 1964]], which destroyed three villages and killed 48 people, was a defining event in the prefecture's modern history. The disaster prompted a comprehensive volcanic monitoring programme and the relocation of the most vulnerable communities, but it also demonstrated the resilience of the Shimabara people and the fertility of the volcanic soils—the ash fall from the eruption produced a burst of agricultural productivity in the following years that became known as the {{nihongo|"Volcano Blessing"|火山の恵み|Kazan no Megumi}}.

== Geography ==

Shimabara Prefecture occupies the volcanic highlands of central Ishijima, stretching from the interior mountains to the borders of [[Ehime Prefecture|Ehime]], [[Shiga Prefecture|Shiga]], [[Tochigi Prefecture|Tochigi]], [[Haruno Prefecture|Haruno]], and [[Noto Prefecture|Noto]]. The prefecture is entirely landlocked and one of the most mountainous on Ishijima, with over 60% of its land area above 500 m elevation.

The [[Shimabara Volcanic Group]] ({{lang|ja|島原火山群}}, ''Shimabara-kazangun'') is the dominant geographic feature. The chain includes three active volcanoes—[[Mount Shimabara]] (1,642 m, last erupted 1964), [[Mount Nosegawa]] (1,388 m, fumarolic activity), and [[Mount Hinoemata]] (1,524 m, dormant)—and a series of dormant peaks, crater lakes, and lava fields that create a dramatic and varied landscape. The [[Shimabara Crater Lake]] ({{lang|ja|島原火口湖}}, ''Shimabara-kakko-ko''), a 2.8 km² emerald-green lake in the caldera of Mount Shimabara, is one of the most photographed natural features in Okaiken.

The volcanic geology produces an abundance of pure, mineral-rich spring water that flows from the mountain slopes in a network of streams and rivers. The [[Shimabara River]] ({{lang|ja|島原川}}, ''Shimabara-gawa''), the prefecture's longest waterway at 72 km, is fed by volcanic springs and maintains a constant temperature of 12–14°C year-round—the ideal conditions for wasabi cultivation.

