== Economy ==

Shimabara Prefecture's economy is built on three pillars: wasabi, sake, and onsen tourism. The volcanic highlands produce conditions that cannot be replicated elsewhere in Okaiken, and the prefecture has leveraged its unique geology into a distinctive economic profile centred on premium agricultural products and geothermal wellness.

=== Wasabi ===
The [[Shimabara wasabi]] ({{lang|ja|島原山葵}}, ''Shimabara-wasabi'') is the prefecture's most important agricultural product and is considered the finest wasabi in Okaiken. The wasabi is cultivated in the {{nihongo|'''Shimabara Wasabi Terraces'''|島原山葵棚田|Shimabara-wasabi tanada}}, a system of stone-walled, terraced pools fed by gravity-directed volcanic spring water that maintains a constant temperature of 12–14°C year-round. The mineral-rich water, the volcanic soils, and the highland fog create growing conditions that produce a wasabi with an exceptionally complex flavour—sweet, pungent, and floral, with a clean, lingering heat that is markedly different from the sharper, more one-dimensional flavour of lowland wasabi.

Shimabara Prefecture produces approximately 680 tonnes of wasabi annually, representing approximately 40% of the national supply. The [[Shimabara Wasabi Cooperative]], based in [[Hinoemata, Shimabara|Hinoemata]], coordinates production for the approximately 600 wasabi farms that maintain the terraces. The [[Shimabara Wasabi Festival]], held in October during the harvest season, draws over 50,000 visitors and features wasabi-tasting competitions, wasabi-grating demonstrations, and the selection of the "Emperor's Root" — the finest specimen of the harvest, which is traditionally presented to the [[Emperor of Okaiken|Emperor]].

=== Sake brewing ===
The volcanic spring water of the Shimabara highlands is ideal for sake brewing, and the prefecture is home to the {{nihongo|'''Shimabara Sake Trail'''|島原酒街道|Shimabara-sake-kaidō}}, a cluster of 18 sake breweries concentrated in the [[Nosegawa]] and [[Kitayama]] valleys. The Shimabara sake style is characterised by its crisp, clean flavour and its mineral finish—the result of the volcanic water, which has a higher mineral content than the softer waters used by the coastal breweries of Noto and Aizawa.

The [[Nosegawa Brewing Company]], founded in 1682, is the oldest continuously operating sake brewery in the prefecture and one of the most respected in Okaiken. The company's flagship product, {{nihongo|"Nosegawa Junmai"|野迫川純米}}, is a premium junmai sake that is served at the [[Imperial Court of Okaiken|Imperial Court]] and is exported to Fosian markets.

=== Onsen tourism ===
The [[Shimabara Onsen]] cluster, a group of 22 hot spring resorts in the volcanic highlands, is the prefecture's most important tourism asset. The volcanic geology produces a range of mineral-rich thermal waters, including the distinctive {{nihongo|"white water"|白湯|shirayu}} springs of [[Nosegawa Onsen]] (sulphur-rich, milky-white water) and the {{nihongo|"iron water"|鉄湯|tetsuyu}} springs of [[Hinoemata Onsen]] (iron-rich, rust-coloured water). The [[Shimabara Onsen Route]], a 45-kilometre trail connecting the major resorts, is one of the most popular wellness tourism circuits in Okaiken.

The [[Shimabara Crater Lake]], accessible by a hiking trail from the Nosegawa Onsen, is a major draw for adventure tourists. The emerald-green lake, set in the caldera of Mount Shimabara, is surrounded by volcanic rock formations and steam vents that create an otherworldly landscape often compared to the volcanic landscapes of [[Dagit]].
Shimabara's economy thrives on volcanic spring resources, specialized sake brewing, and coastal maritime services. Spring-water gravel beds in the highland valleys yield slow-grown wasabi roots noted for their sharp pungency and delicate sweetness. Regional breweries draw on mineral-filtered volcanic snowmelt and heritage koji strains to produce dry ginjo sake, while coastal harbor facilities host emergency tugboat stations and commercial diving cooperatives that service passing freight vessels in the western straits.

== Culture ==

=== Cuisine ===
Shimabara Prefecture's cuisine is defined by wasabi and volcanic spring water. The signature dish is {{nihongo|'''Shimabara wasabi-don'''|島原山葵丟|Shimabara-wasabi don}}, a bowl of steamed rice topped with freshly grated wasabi, sliced sashimi, and a drizzle of soy sauce—a deceptively simple preparation that showcases the quality of the prefecture's wasabi. The dish is served at the wasabi terraces during the harvest season, where visitors can select their own wasabi root and have it grated fresh.

Other local specialties include ''onsen tamago'' (eggs slow-cooked in the volcanic hot springs, producing a custard-like texture), ''Shimabara soba'' (buckwheat noodles served in a wasabi-infused broth), and ''volcano curry'' (a spicy curry rice flavoured with wasabi and served in a bowl shaped like the Shimabara Crater Lake—a popular dish at the onsen resorts).

=== Volcanic culture ===
The [[Shimabara Volcano Festival]] ({{lang|ja|島原火山祭}}, ''Shimabara-kazan Matsuri''), held annually in August, is the prefecture's largest cultural event, drawing over 120,000 visitors. The festival celebrates the volcanic landscape that defines Shimabara, with fire performances, lava-lamp installations, and a ceremonial offering of the year's first wasabi harvest to the volcanic deities. The festival also includes a volcanic science exhibition and a guided hike to the Crater Lake, which is normally restricted during the eruption risk season.

== Transportation ==

=== Rail ===
The [[Shimabara Line]] connects [[Shimabara Station]] to the [[Ishijima Shinkansen]] network at [[Ehime Station]], providing high-speed rail access to [[Okami]] and [[Toyozawa]]. The [[Volcano Loop Line]], a conventional rail service, circles the Shimabara Volcanic Group and connects the interior towns and onsen resorts.

=== Road ===
The [[Shimabara Expressway]] is the primary road link, connecting the prefectural capital to the [[Ishijima Coastal Expressway]] at the Ehime border. The [[Crater Lake Road]], a scenic mountain highway that ascends to the Shimabara Crater Lake, is one of the most celebrated driving routes in Okaiken but is frequently closed during the wet season due to volcanic gas emissions and landslides.

== Education ==

The [[Shimabara Volcanological Institute]] is the prefecture's premier research institution, specialising in volcanic monitoring, geothermal energy, and the mineralogy of volcanic spring water. The institute operates the [[Shimabara Volcano Observatory]] on Mount Shimabara and provides real-time eruption monitoring for the national volcanic early warning system. The [[Shimabara Wasabi Research Station]], based in [[Hinoemata, Shimabara|Hinoemata]], is the nation's leading centre for wasabi cultivation research.

